\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{The Life Cycle of Stars}
Stars are born within vast clouds of gas and dust known as nebulae. Gravity pulls these materials together, and as the cloud collapses, temperatures at its core rise dramatically. When the temperature reaches approximately ten million Kelvin, nuclear fusion ignites. The star enters what astronomers call the main sequence, where it will spend the majority of its life converting hydrogen into helium. Our own Sun has been burning steadily on the main sequence for roughly 4.6 billion years and will continue to do so for another five billion.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\begin{document}
\section{Black Holes}
Black holes represent one of the most extreme predictions of Einstein's general theory of relativity. At their centers lies a singularity, a point where density becomes infinite and the known laws of physics break down. Surrounding the singularity is the event horizon, the boundary beyond which nothing — not even light — can escape. Stellar-mass black holes form when massive stars exhaust their nuclear fuel and collapse under their own gravity. Supermassive black holes, containing millions or even billions of solar masses, lurk at the centers of most large galaxies, including our own Milky Way.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{The Expanding Universe}
In 1929, Edwin Hubble published observations that would forever change our understanding of the cosmos. He discovered that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and that the farther a galaxy is, the faster it recedes. This relationship, now known as Hubble's Law, provided compelling evidence that the universe is expanding. Extrapolating this expansion backward in time leads to the conclusion that everything originated from an extremely hot, dense state roughly 13.8 billion years ago — the Big Bang. Remarkably, observations in 1998 revealed that this expansion is not slowing down but accelerating, driven by a mysterious force called dark energy.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{Neutron Stars and Pulsars}
When a massive star dies in a supernova explosion, it can leave behind an extraordinarily dense remnant called a neutron star. These objects pack roughly 1.4 solar masses into a sphere only about 20 kilometers in diameter. The density is staggering — a teaspoon of neutron star material would weigh approximately one billion tons on Earth. Some neutron stars rotate incredibly rapidly, emitting beams of electromagnetic radiation that sweep across space like cosmic lighthouses. When these beams periodically sweep past Earth, we detect regular pulses of radio waves, and we call these objects pulsars. The first pulsar was discovered in 1967 by Jocelyn Bell Burnell.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{Planetary Formation}
Planets form from the leftover material surrounding a young star in a rotating disk of gas and dust called a protoplanetary disk. Tiny dust grains collide and stick together, gradually building larger and larger bodies called planetesimals. Over millions of years, these planetesimals accrete into protoplanets through countless collisions. Rocky planets like Earth form in the inner regions of the disk, where temperatures are too high for volatile compounds to condense. Gas giants like Jupiter form farther out, where ices can contribute to the growing cores and trigger rapid accumulation of surrounding gas. The entire process can take tens of millions of years.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{hyperref}
\begin{document}
\section{Galaxies and Their Structures}
Galaxies are the fundamental building blocks of the large-scale structure of the universe. They come in a remarkable variety of shapes and sizes, from elegant spiral galaxies like the Milky Way to smooth elliptical galaxies containing trillions of stars. Irregular galaxies, often shaped by gravitational interactions with neighbors, defy simple classification. A typical galaxy contains anywhere from millions to hundreds of billions of stars, along with gas, dust, and vast amounts of invisible dark matter. Galaxies are not isolated islands — they cluster together under gravity into groups, clusters, and superclusters that form the cosmic web threading throughout the observable universe.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{Gravitational Waves}
For a century after Einstein proposed general relativity, gravitational waves remained a theoretical prediction awaiting confirmation. These ripples in the fabric of spacetime are generated by some of the most violent events in the universe. The first direct detection came on September 14, 2015, when the LIGO observatories recorded distortions smaller than one-thousandth the diameter of a proton. The signal had traveled over a billion light-years from the merger of two black holes. This discovery opened an entirely new window onto the universe, allowing astronomers to observe cosmic events that produce no light whatsoever. The scientists responsible were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2017.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{The Search for Exoplanets}
For most of human history, we had no direct evidence that planets existed beyond our solar system. That changed dramatically in 1992 with the discovery of planets orbiting a pulsar, and again in 1995 when the first exoplanet around a Sun-like star was confirmed. Since then, the field has exploded. NASA's Kepler Space Telescope alone identified over 2,600 confirmed exoplanets during its mission. Astronomers now estimate that virtually every star in the Milky Way hosts at least one planet. The most exciting frontier is the identification of potentially habitable worlds — rocky planets orbiting within the so-called habitable zone where liquid water could exist on the surface.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{Dark Matter}
Approximately 85 percent of all matter in the universe is invisible. We cannot see it, and it does not interact with light in any known way. Yet its gravitational influence is unmistakable. Galaxies rotate at speeds that would fling them apart if only visible matter were present, and the bending of light around galaxy clusters is far stronger than ordinary matter can explain. This unseen substance, called dark matter, holds galaxies and galaxy clusters together. Despite decades of searching, physicists have not yet identified what dark matter actually is. Leading candidates include weakly interacting massive particles, or WIMPs, and axions, but none have been directly detected in laboratory experiments.
\end{document}

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{The Cosmic Microwave Background}
The cosmic microwave background, or CMB, is the oldest light we can observe — a faint glow of microwave radiation permeating the entire sky. It was released approximately 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when the universe cooled enough for hydrogen atoms to form and photons could finally travel freely. Accidentally discovered in 1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, the CMB has since been mapped with extraordinary precision by satellites including COBE, WMAP, and Planck. Tiny temperature fluctuations in the CMB, at the level of one part in one hundred thousand, reveal the seeds of all cosmic structure. These primordial density variations eventually grew into the galaxies, clusters, and filaments we observe today.
\end{document}

