\title{Advances in Metal-Organic Frameworks: Synthesis, Properties, and Applications}
\author{Prof. Yuki Tanaka\\Institute of Materials Chemistry, Kyoto University}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as one of the most vibrant research areas
in materials chemistry. This review surveys developments from 2021 to 2024, covering
new synthesis strategies, functionalization approaches, and applications in gas storage,
separation, drug delivery, and heterogeneous catalysis. Special attention is given to
stable MOFs capable of operating under humid conditions and to the emerging field of
dynamic or responsive MOFs that change their structure upon external stimuli.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Metal-organic frameworks are crystalline porous materials constructed from metal
nodes (secondary building units, SBUs) connected by organic linkers. Their defining
characteristic is an extraordinarily high surface area—often exceeding \SI{7000}{m^2/g}—
combined with a chemically tunable interior environment. Since the seminal work of
Yaghi, Kitagawa, and Férey in the 1990s, over 100,000 distinct MOF structures have
been synthesized and deposited in the Cambridge Structural Database.

Recent years have seen the field mature from curiosity-driven synthesis to
applications-oriented design, with several MOF-based products reaching commercialization.

\section{New Synthesis Strategies}
\subsection{Mechanochemical Synthesis}
Grinding metal salts with organic linkers in the absence of solvent has proven
a powerful route to MOF synthesis. This approach eliminates solvent waste, reduces
reaction times from days to minutes, and often yields products with improved
crystallinity compared to solvothermal methods.

\subsection{Continuous Flow Synthesis}
Microfluidic and continuous flow reactors enable precise control of nucleation
and growth kinetics, yielding MOF nanoparticles with narrow size distributions
suitable for biomedical applications. Recent work from the Farha group demonstrated
the continuous synthesis of UiO-66 nanoparticles with mean diameters of
\SI{80 \pm 12}{nm}.

\subsection{Post-Synthetic Modification}
PSM strategies allow introduction of functional groups that would be incompatible
with direct synthesis conditions. Recent advances include:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Metalation of open-metal sites for catalysis
  \item Click chemistry for drug conjugation
  \item Atomic layer deposition to deposit catalytic nanoclusters within pores
\end{enumerate}

\section{Stability Improvements}
Water stability has long been a limitation of MOF technology. Zirconium-based
MOFs (UiO series) exhibit exceptional stability due to the high charge density
of \ch{Zr^{4+}} and the resulting strong metal-linker bonds. Recent work has
extended this principle to hafnium and cerium analogs with even greater stability.

Defect engineering has emerged as a counterintuitive strategy: controlled introduction
of linker vacancies not only maintains structural integrity but enhances gas uptake
by creating additional open metal sites.

\section{Gas Storage and Separation}
\subsection{Carbon Capture}
MOFs functionalized with amine groups achieve \ch{CO2} uptake capacities exceeding
\SI{4.0}{mmol/g} at \SI{0.15}{bar} and \SI{298}{K}, conditions relevant to flue gas
capture. The chemisorption mechanism involves formation of carbamic acid:
\begin{equation}
\ch{R-NH2 + CO2 -> R-NH-COOH}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Hydrogen Storage}
The US DOE target of \SI{6.5}{wt\%} gravimetric hydrogen capacity remains challenging.
Recent ultraporous MOFs (NU-1501-Al, surface area \SI{7310}{m^2/g}) achieve capacities
of \SI{14.0}{wt\%} at \SI{77}{K}, approaching the thermodynamic limit for purely
physisorptive systems.

\section{Drug Delivery}
MOF nanoparticles functionalized with targeting ligands offer pH-responsive drug
release in tumor microenvironments. The degradation of ZIF-8 at endosomal pH
(approximately 5.5) releases encapsulated therapeutics selectively within cancer cells,
avoiding systemic toxicity associated with conventional chemotherapy.

\section{Photocatalysis}
Titanium-based MOFs (MIL-125) absorb UV light and generate electron-hole pairs
capable of reducing \ch{CO2} to formate and methanol. Recent work incorporating
ruthenium bipyridyl photosensitizers extended light absorption into the visible
spectrum, improving solar energy utilization significantly.

\section{Outlook}
The next frontier in MOF research lies in dynamic frameworks that respond to
light, heat, or chemical signals, and in the scale-up of manufacturing processes
for industrial deployment. Computational high-throughput screening of hypothetical
MOF databases now routinely identifies promising candidates for experimental synthesis.

