\title{CRISPR-Inspired Chemical Biology: Nucleic Acid Chemistry and Beyond}
\author{Dr. Priya Nair\\School of Chemical Biology, Indian Institute of Science}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The intersection of chemistry and molecular biology has produced some of the most
transformative tools of the twenty-first century. This review focuses on recent
chemical advances that underpin and extend CRISPR-Cas9 technology, including
nucleotide analogs that enhance guide RNA stability, small-molecule modulators
of Cas9 activity, and entirely synthetic gene-editing systems based on programmable
DNA-binding scaffolds. We also discuss the emerging chemistry of base editing
and prime editing.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Cluster regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR) and the associated
Cas9 endonuclease have revolutionized genetics, medicine, and agriculture. However,
the technology as initially described relied on biological components with inherent
limitations: immunogenicity, off-target activity, and poor delivery to certain
tissue types. Chemistry has played a central role in addressing each of these
challenges.

The synthetic chemistry community has responded with a wave of innovation spanning
nucleotide synthesis, bioconjugation, and the development of entirely synthetic
alternatives to CRISPR components.

\section{Modified Guide RNAs}
\subsection{Backbone Modifications}
Phosphorothioate (PS) modifications at the 3' and 5' termini of single guide RNAs
(sgRNAs) significantly improve nuclease resistance without perturbing Cas9 binding.
The substitution of a non-bridging oxygen with sulfur:
\begin{equation}
\text{P-O-R} \rightarrow \text{P-S-R}
\end{equation}
increases the half-life of sgRNAs in biological fluids from minutes to several hours,
a critical improvement for in vivo applications.

\subsection{2'-O-Methyl and 2'-F Modifications}
Introduction of 2'-O-methyl and 2'-fluoro groups at specific positions within the
seed region of the spacer sequence reduces off-target editing by 50--90\% in
clinical models while maintaining on-target efficiency. This remarkable selectivity
improvement arises from the reduced flexibility of modified duplexes.

\section{Small-Molecule Modulators}
The ability to control Cas9 activity with small molecules represents a significant
advance for therapeutic applications where temporal control of editing is desirable.

\subsection{Anti-CRISPR Small Molecules}
Recently, several small molecules have been identified that inhibit Cas9 by blocking
the guide RNA binding groove. High-throughput screening campaigns have identified
compounds with IC$_{50}$ values in the nanomolar range against both SpCas9 and SaCas9.

\subsection{Photocaged Guide RNAs}
Caging groups installed on critical nucleobases render guide RNAs inactive until
photolysis at \SI{365}{nm}. This approach enables spatiotemporal control of gene
editing in live animals and has been applied to zebrafish developmental biology.

\section{Base Editing}
Base editors developed by the Liu laboratory convert one DNA base to another without
double-strand breaks. Chemical deamination:
\begin{equation}
\text{C} \xrightarrow{\text{APOBEC}} \text{U} \rightarrow \text{T}
\end{equation}
can correct point mutations responsible for genetic diseases. Recent fourth-generation
base editors (ABE8e) achieve adenine-to-inosine conversion with near-perfect efficiency
and dramatically reduced off-target RNA editing.

\section{Prime Editing}
Prime editing, introduced in 2019, combines a nickase Cas9 with an engineered reverse
transcriptase and a prime editing guide RNA (pegRNA) to write new genetic information
directly into the genome. Chemical improvements to pegRNA include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Engineered pegRNAs (epegRNAs):} Addition of structured 3' RNA
        motifs that prevent degradation by cellular exonucleases.
  \item \textbf{Chemically modified pegRNAs:} Combination of PS, 2'-OMe, and
        locked nucleic acid (LNA) modifications.
\end{itemize}

\section{Fully Synthetic Alternatives}
Peptide nucleic acids (PNAs) and gamma-PNAs with modified backbones can invade
duplex DNA and direct homologous recombination. Unlike CRISPR, these fully synthetic
systems are non-immunogenic and can be manufactured with complete chemical control.

\section{Delivery Chemistry}
Lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) encapsulating mRNA encoding Cas9 have entered clinical
trials for transthyretin amyloidosis. Ionizable lipids with optimized pKa values
(6.2--6.8) achieve efficient endosomal escape while minimizing liver toxicity.

\section{Conclusions}
The marriage of synthetic chemistry with CRISPR technology has dramatically expanded
the scope and safety of gene editing. The next decade will likely see fully chemically
synthesized gene-editing tools with programmable specificity and controlled
pharmacodynamics.

