\title{Two-Dimensional Materials Chemistry: Synthesis and Functionalization of Beyond-Graphene Systems}
\author{Prof. Lihua Wang\\Department of Materials Science, Tsinghua University}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The discovery of graphene launched an era of two-dimensional (2D) materials research
that has since expanded to include transition metal dichalcogenides, MXenes, hexagonal
boron nitride, and covalent organic frameworks. This review surveys the synthetic
chemistry underpinning recent discoveries in 2D materials, focusing on liquid-phase
exfoliation, chemical vapor deposition, and bottom-up molecular assembly. Applications
in energy storage, catalysis, and sensing are highlighted alongside emerging challenges
in defect control and large-area synthesis.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Two-dimensional materials confine electrons to a single atomic plane, producing
phenomenological properties absent in their bulk counterparts. Graphene's remarkable
mobility (\SI{200000}{cm^2/(V.s)}) and mechanical strength (\SI{130}{GPa}) motivated
considerable effort to discover analogous properties in other chemical systems.
As of 2024, the 2D materials family encompasses over 1000 experimentally realized
compounds and many thousands predicted by computational methods.

\section{Transition Metal Dichalcogenides}
\subsection{Chemical Vapor Deposition}
CVD synthesis of \ch{MoS2}, \ch{WS2}, and \ch{MoSe2} monolayers has advanced
remarkably. Epitaxial growth on sapphire substrates now routinely produces
wafer-scale monolayers with domain sizes exceeding \SI{1}{cm} and carrier
mobilities of \SI{100}{cm^2/(V.s)}.

Precise control of stoichiometry is achieved through the ratio of chalcogen
precursor pressure to metal precursor:
\begin{equation}
\ch{MoCl5 + S -> MoS2 + HCl}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Phase Engineering}
The semiconducting 2H phase and metallic 1T phase of \ch{MoS2} differ only in
the stacking arrangement of sulfur layers. Chemical reduction with n-butyllithium
drives the 2H-to-1T transition, producing a metallic phase useful for electrocatalysis.
Controlled oxidation restores the semiconducting phase.

\section{MXenes}
MXenes, with general formula \ch{M_{n+1}X_nT_x} where M is an early transition
metal, X is carbon or nitrogen, and $T_x$ represents surface terminations
(\ch{-OH}, \ch{-O}, \ch{-F}), have emerged as exceptional candidates for
energy storage applications.

\subsection{Synthesis by Selective Etching}
MXenes are synthesized by selective etching of the A layers from MAX phases:
\begin{equation}
\ch{Ti3AlC2 + HF -> Ti3C2T_x + AlF3 + H2}
\end{equation}
Minimally intensive layer delamination (MILD) etching using LiF/HCl avoids
the hazards of concentrated HF while producing high-quality single-layer MXenes.

\subsection{Surface Chemistry}
The surface terminations profoundly influence electronic properties. DFT calculations
and XPS studies confirm that \ch{-O} terminated \ch{Ti3C2} exhibits metallic
conductivity (\SI{8000}{S/cm}) while \ch{-OH} terminated samples are more resistive.

\section{Hexagonal Boron Nitride}
\ch{h-BN} serves as the ideal dielectric substrate for graphene and TMD devices.
Epitaxial growth of \ch{h-BN} on copper foils at \SI{1050}{\celsius} under borazine
atmosphere produces atomically flat monolayers with RMS roughness below \SI{0.1}{nm}.

\section{Covalent Organic Frameworks in 2D Form}
Two-dimensional COFs assembled from planar building blocks via imine condensation
or boronate ester formation exhibit long-range crystalline order.
\begin{equation}
\text{Ar-CHO} + \text{Ar-NH}_2 \rightarrow \text{Ar-CH=N-Ar} + \text{H}_2\text{O}
\end{equation}
Recent work demonstrates that 2D COF thin films with pore sizes of \SI{2.4}{nm}
function as nanofiltration membranes with water permeance exceeding
\SI{2000}{L/(m^2.h.bar)}.

\section{Defect Engineering}
Point defects in 2D materials serve as catalytic active sites, single-photon emitters,
and spin qubits. Controlled introduction of sulfur vacancies in \ch{MoS2} via
archethanolamine treatment creates active sites for hydrogen evolution with
overpotentials as low as \SI{110}{mV} at \SI{10}{mA/cm^2}.

\section{Conclusions}
The 2D materials field continues to expand at a remarkable pace, driven by the
richness of chemical space available and the unique properties that emerge at the
monolayer limit. Key challenges include scalable synthesis without sacrificing
quality, controlled doping, and integration with existing semiconductor manufacturing.

