\title{Electrocatalytic Carbon Dioxide Reduction: Recent Progress and Perspectives}
\author{Dr. Mei-Ling Chen and Prof. Andrei Popescu\\Institute for Electrochemistry, ETH Zurich}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Electrocatalytic reduction of carbon dioxide (\ch{CO2}RR) to value-added chemicals
and fuels represents a critical strategy for closing the anthropogenic carbon cycle.
This review covers recent progress in catalyst design, mechanistic understanding,
and system-level engineering for \ch{CO2}RR. We highlight the discovery of
new single-atom catalysts, molecular electrocatalysts, and tandem systems achieving
Faradaic efficiencies above 90\% for multi-electron products such as ethylene,
ethanol, and formate.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The atmospheric concentration of \ch{CO2} has exceeded \SI{420}{ppm}, surpassing
levels not seen for at least 800,000 years. While carbon capture and storage provides
a partial solution, the electrochemical conversion of \ch{CO2} to useful chemicals
using renewable electricity closes the carbon cycle productively.

The thermodynamic minimum potential for \ch{CO2} reduction to various products
ranges from \SI{-0.17}{V} vs. RHE (formate) to \SI{-0.25}{V} (ethanol), but
practical electrocatalysts require substantial overpotentials due to sluggish
kinetics and competing hydrogen evolution.

\section{Copper Catalysts for Multi-Carbon Products}
Copper is unique among metals in its ability to catalyze \ch{CO2} reduction to
multi-carbon (C$_{2+}$) products. Recent advances have focused on controlling
copper's surface structure at the atomic level.

\subsection{Oxide-Derived Copper}
Copper prepared by reduction of \ch{Cu2O} exhibits a roughened surface with
abundant grain boundaries. These grain boundaries create high concentrations of
undercoordinated sites that favor \ch{CO} coupling:
\begin{equation}
2\,\ch{CO^*} \rightarrow \ch{OC-CO^*} \rightarrow \text{C}_{2+}\text{ products}
\end{equation}
Faradaic efficiencies of \SI{70}{\%} toward ethylene have been reported on oxide-derived
copper at \SI{-1.0}{V} vs. RHE in alkaline electrolyte.

\subsection{Copper Nanocubes}
Exposing predominantly the (100) facet of copper, nanocubes produced by polyol
synthesis favor formation of ethylene over ethanol. The (100)/(111) selectivity
ratio is attributed to the optimal \ch{CO} binding energy on (100) terraces that
balances coverage and reactivity.

\section{Single-Atom Catalysts}
Single-atom catalysts (SACs) consisting of isolated metal centers on nitrogen-doped
carbon (M-N-C) have emerged as highly selective \ch{CO2}RR electrocatalysts.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
