\title{Supramolecular Chemistry: Self-Assembly and Function at the Nanoscale}
\author{Dr. Isabelle Leblanc\\Laboratoire de Chimie Supramoléculaire, Université de Strasbourg}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Supramolecular chemistry exploits non-covalent interactions to create complex,
function-rich architectures from simpler molecular components. This review covers
developments from 2021 to 2024 in self-assembled cages, dynamic covalent chemistry,
mechanically interlocked molecules, and biological supramolecular systems. Emerging
applications in catalysis, molecular machines, and drug encapsulation are discussed
alongside fundamental advances in understanding recognition and self-sorting phenomena.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The twelve principles articulated by Jean-Marie Lehn in his 1987 Nobel Lecture
remain the guiding framework for supramolecular chemistry: information, complementarity,
preorganization, and cooperativity govern the formation of molecular assemblies.
Four decades of subsequent research have transformed these conceptual principles
into a practical toolkit for molecular engineering.

Recent years have witnessed the construction of increasingly complex architectures
with functions that rival those of proteins and other biological macromolecules.

\section{Self-Assembled Metal-Organic Cages}
Subcomponent self-assembly, pioneered by Nitschke and colleagues, enables rapid
construction of geometrically precise cages from metal ions, amines, and aldehydes.
The simultaneous formation of metal-ligand bonds and imine bonds creates systems
where thermodynamic control yields a single discrete product from dozens of
potential isomers.

\subsection{Chiral Cages for Enantioselective Catalysis}
A \ch{Fe8L12} cube with chiral panels selectively encapsulates one enantiomer
of guest molecules with enantioselectivities up to 93\% ee. Catalytic reactions
performed inside the cage cavity proceed with remarkable stereoselectivity because
the confined microenvironment enforces a specific approach trajectory.

\subsection{Stimuli-Responsive Cages}
Cages incorporating light-sensitive azo groups undergo photoisomerization between
expanded and contracted states, enabling light-gated encapsulation and release of
guest molecules. This principle has been applied to the photocontrolled delivery
of drugs to cancer cell lines in vitro.

\section{Dynamic Covalent Chemistry}
Dynamic covalent bonds—reversible under thermodynamic control but stable under
ambient conditions—bridge the gap between covalent and supramolecular chemistry.
Key reactions include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Imine exchange (reversible in acid or at elevated temperature)
  \item Disulfide metathesis (reversible under redox control)
  \item Boronate ester formation and exchange (reversible at pH$<$7)
  \item Diels-Alder/retro-Diels-Alder equilibria (reversible at high temperature)
\end{itemize}

Covalent organic frameworks built from dynamic bonds are thermodynamically stable
yet can undergo repair of structural defects, a property with implications for
self-healing materials.

\section{Mechanically Interlocked Molecules}
\subsection{Rotaxanes as Molecular Machines}
Rotaxanes consist of a dumbbell-shaped molecule threaded through a macrocyclic
wheel. Redox-active stations along the dumbbell control the position of the wheel
through changes in electrostatic interactions. Recent work by Stoddart's group
demonstrated a rotaxane-based artificial molecular pump that transports molecules
against a concentration gradient.

\subsection{Catenanes in Logic Gates}
Interconvertible co-conformers of bistable catenanes respond to chemical inputs
(pH, redox, light) and can be wired together to implement Boolean logic functions,
a step toward molecular computing.

\section{Hydrogen Bond-Directed Assembly}
Chiral bis-urea compounds in apolar solvents form helical tubular assemblies
through a cooperative network of eight hydrogen bonds per unit. These nanotubes
selectively transport chloride ions across lipid bilayers with turnover numbers
exceeding \SI{e6}{s^{-1}}.

\section{Biological Supramolecular Systems}
Virus-like particles assembled from engineered protein subunits have been loaded
with catalytic RNA (ribozymes) for therapeutic applications. The icosahedral
symmetry of these 60-subunit assemblies provides an ideal scaffold for displaying
targeting ligands at precise geometrical positions.

\section{Outlook}
The merger of supramolecular chemistry with machine learning for structure prediction
and with synthetic biology for the production of complex protein assemblies represents
the most exciting frontier. The realization of truly functional molecular machines
operating in cellular environments remains a grand challenge.

