\title{Perovskite Chemistry: From Solar Cells to Light-Emitting Diodes}
\author{Prof. Carlos Mendoza\\Department of Physical Chemistry, Universidad Autónoma de Madrid}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Halide perovskites with the general formula \ch{ABX3} (A = organic cation or Cs;
B = Pb, Sn; X = I, Br, Cl) have achieved certified power conversion efficiencies
exceeding 26\% in single-junction solar cells, approaching the Shockley-Queisser limit.
This review examines the synthetic chemistry underpinning recent efficiency records,
stability improvements through compositional engineering and passivation strategies,
and the extension of perovskite chemistry to light-emitting diodes and photodetectors.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The ABX$_3$ perovskite structure was first described for calcium titanate in 1945,
but its relevance to photovoltaics emerged only with the 2009 report by Miyasaka
et al. demonstrating \SI{3.8}{\%} efficiency in a dye-sensitized solar cell architecture.
Subsequent rapid development has compressed the traditional efficiency improvement
curve from decades to years.

The exceptional performance of halide perovskites arises from their defect-tolerant
electronic structure, tunable bandgap, and ambipolar charge transport. However,
lead toxicity, moisture sensitivity, and phase instability remain impediments to
commercialization.

\section{Compositional Engineering}
\subsection{Mixed Cation Formulations}
The most efficient perovskite compositions employ mixed A-site cations:
\begin{equation}
\ch{(FA_{0.83}MA_{0.17})_{0.95}Cs_{0.05}PbI_{2.55}Br_{0.45}}
\end{equation}
where FA = formamidinium and MA = methylammonium. The inclusion of Cs\textsuperscript{+}
suppresses the photoinactive $\delta$-FAPbI$_3$ phase by inducing a compression of
the tolerance factor toward unity.

\subsection{Lead Reduction Strategies}
Toxicity concerns motivate research into tin-based perovskites:
\begin{equation}
\ch{FASnI3}: E_g = \SI{1.41}{eV}, \mu_h = \SI{300}{cm^2/(V.s)}
\end{equation}
The principal challenge is the facile oxidation of \ch{Sn^{2+}} to \ch{Sn^{4+}}.
Reducing agents such as hydrazine and tin fluoride have been employed to maintain
the desired oxidation state, with champion efficiencies reaching \SI{15.4}{\%}.

\section{Interface Passivation}
Defects at grain boundaries and the perovskite/transport layer interface act as
non-radiative recombination centers, limiting open-circuit voltage. Several passivation
strategies have been demonstrated:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Lewis base passivation:} Molecules with lone electron pairs
        (thiophene, pyridine, PCBA) coordinate to undercoordinated Pb sites.
  \item \textbf{Halide passivation:} Post-deposition treatment with methylammonium
        iodide fills iodide vacancies.
  \item \textbf{Bifunctional molecules:} Molecules bearing both amino and carboxylic
        acid groups form ordered monolayers at grain boundaries.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Stability Improvements}
\subsection{Encapsulation}
Barrier layers based on atomic layer deposited \ch{Al2O3} and \ch{TiO2} reduce
moisture ingress by three orders of magnitude. Photostability under continuous
AM1.5G illumination at \SI{85}{\celsius} exceeding 1000 hours is now routinely
reported for encapsulated devices.

\subsection{2D/3D Heterostructures}
Layered Ruddlesden-Popper perovskites of the form \ch{(RNH3)2(MA)_{n-1}Pb_nI_{3n+1}}
are hydrophobic and can be deposited on 3D perovskite absorbers to create
moisture-resistant passivating overlayers. The 2D capping layer provides
stability while the 3D bulk maintains high carrier mobility.

\section{Perovskite LEDs}
Perovskite light-emitting diodes (PeLEDs) have achieved external quantum efficiencies
(EQE) of \SI{28.9}{\%} in the near-infrared and \SI{23.4}{\%} in the green.
The emission wavelength is tunable from \SI{410}{nm} to \SI{780}{nm} by varying
the halide composition, enabling display applications with pure-color emission.

\section{Perovskite Tandem Solar Cells}
Monolithic perovskite/silicon tandem cells have achieved certified efficiencies
of \SI{33.9}{\%}, exceeding the single-junction silicon record. The wide-bandgap
(\SI{1.68}{eV}) perovskite top cell captures high-energy photons while the silicon
bottom cell harvests the remainder.

\section{Conclusions and Outlook}
Halide perovskite photovoltaics have demonstrated a trajectory without precedent
in solar cell history. The key challenges of lead toxicity and long-term stability
must be resolved for widespread deployment. The perovskite LED field is poised
to challenge organic LED technology in display applications within the next five years.

