\title{Medicinal Chemistry in the Age of Targeted Protein Degradation}
\author{Prof. Amelia Fontaine\\Institute of Pharmaceutical Sciences, University of Geneva}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Targeted protein degradation (TPD) represents a paradigm shift in drug discovery,
enabling elimination of previously undruggable proteins through the cell's own
ubiquitin-proteasome system. This review covers recent advances in proteolysis
targeting chimeras (PROTACs), molecular glues, LYTACs, and AUTACs. We examine
the medicinal chemistry principles governing PROTAC design—including ternary complex
formation, linker optimization, and cell permeability—and survey the clinical
pipeline of degrader compounds.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conventional small-molecule drugs function by occupying the active site or allosteric
pocket of a target protein, exerting pharmacological effect only while the drug
is bound (occupancy-driven pharmacology). This approach requires maintaining adequate
drug concentrations and is limited to proteins with defined binding pockets.

Targeted protein degradation instead redirects the cell's quality control machinery
to degrade specific proteins. A single degrader molecule can catalytically eliminate
multiple copies of the target—an event-driven pharmacology—and can in principle
drug any protein in the proteome.

\section{PROTAC Mechanism and Design}
PROTACs are heterobifunctional molecules consisting of three components:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A \textbf{warhead} that binds the target protein of interest (POI)
  \item A \textbf{linker} connecting the two binding moieties
  \item An \textbf{E3 ligase ligand} (VHL, CRBN, MDM2, cIAP1)
\end{enumerate}

The PROTAC simultaneously engages the POI and an E3 ubiquitin ligase, forming a
ternary complex that juxtaposes the E3 with the target, enabling ubiquitin transfer
and subsequent proteasomal degradation:
\begin{equation}
\text{E3} + \text{PROTAC} + \text{POI} \rightleftharpoons
[\text{E3:PROTAC:POI}] \rightarrow \text{Ub-POI} \rightarrow \text{Degradation}
\end{equation}

\subsection{E3 Ligase Ligands}
Cereblone (CRBN) recruited by pomalidomide analogs and von Hippel-Lindau (VHL)
recruited by hydroxyproline peptide mimetics are the most widely used E3 ligase
binders in PROTAC design. CRBN-based PROTACs have entered the clinic for targets
including BRD4, BTK, and androgen receptor.

\subsection{Linker Design}
Linker length, flexibility, and chemical composition profoundly influence PROTAC
activity. Polyethylene glycol (PEG) linkers confer aqueous solubility while alkyl
linkers provide more rigid geometry. Recent work using structure-based design and
molecular dynamics simulation has enabled rational linker optimization.

\section{Molecular Glues}
Molecular glues stabilize protein-protein interactions between a target and an E3
ligase without a bifunctional structure. Thalidomide and its immunomodulatory drug
(IMiD) derivatives are the prototypical molecular glues, redirecting CRBN to
degrade neosubstrates including Ikaros, Aiolos, and GSPT1.

Recent high-throughput degrader screens have identified novel molecular glues for
oncogenic targets. Cyclin K degraders discovered in phenotypic screens inhibit
cancer cell growth at nanomolar concentrations.

\section{LYTACs}
Lysosome-targeting chimeras (LYTACs) use cell-surface receptors (CI-M6PR,
ASGPR) to direct extracellular and membrane proteins to lysosomal degradation.
Glycopeptide ligands conjugated to antibodies or small molecules direct the LYTAC
to receptor-mediated endocytosis:
\begin{equation}
\text{Target} + \text{LYTAC} \rightarrow \text{Ternary Complex} \rightarrow
\text{Endosome} \rightarrow \text{Lysosomal Degradation}
\end{equation}

\section{AUTACs and ATTECs}
Autophagy-targeting chimeras (AUTACs) and autophagy-tethering compounds (ATTECs)
bring target proteins or protein aggregates into contact with the autophagy machinery
for lysosomal elimination. These approaches are particularly promising for degradation
of toxic protein aggregates associated with neurodegeneration.

\section{Clinical Landscape}
As of 2024, over 20 PROTAC/degrader compounds are in clinical trials. ARV-471,
a PROTAC targeting estrogen receptor, demonstrated a clinical benefit rate of 50\%
in heavily pretreated ER+ breast cancer. CFT8919 targets EGFR with exon 20 insertions,
a mutation historically resistant to small-molecule inhibitors.

\section{Challenges and Outlook}
Major challenges include the large molecular weight of PROTACs (typically 800--1000 Da)
that limits oral bioavailability, the prevalence of "hook effect" at high concentrations,
and tissue-specific E3 ligase expression. The next frontier involves discovery of
new E3 ligase handles that expand the druggable E3 ligasome and development of
PROTACs suitable for CNS penetration.

