\title{Battery Chemistry: From Lithium-Ion to Solid-State and Beyond}
\author{Dr. Fatima Al-Hassan and Prof. Nikolai Voronov\\Center for Electrochemical Energy Storage, KAUST}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Electrochemical energy storage underpins the transition to renewable energy and
electrified transportation. This review examines recent developments in lithium-ion
battery chemistry, the chemistry of solid electrolytes, lithium-sulfur batteries,
and sodium-ion batteries. We discuss the fundamental electrochemistry of electrode
materials, electrolyte decomposition pathways and SEI formation, and the molecular-
level understanding necessary to design next-generation energy storage systems.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The lithium-ion battery (LIB), commercialized by Sony in 1991 based on work by
Goodenough, Whittingham, and Yoshino (2019 Nobel Laureates), transformed consumer
electronics and now enables the electrification of transportation. However, the energy
density of conventional LIBs (\SI{250}{Wh/kg}) is approaching its practical limit
and cannot meet the demands of long-range electric aviation.

Chemical innovation at every component—cathode, anode, electrolyte, separator—is
necessary to realize the next generation of batteries.

\section{Advanced Cathode Materials}
\subsection{Ni-Rich NMC}
Nickel-rich layered oxides \ch{Li[Ni_xMn_yCo_z]O2} (NMC, $x > 0.8$) offer specific
capacities above \SI{200}{mAh/g} but suffer from structural instability at high
states of charge. Concentration-gradient cathode particles with Ni-rich cores and
Mn-rich surfaces achieve improved thermal stability while maintaining high capacity.

\subsection{Lithium-Rich Oxides}
Lithium-rich layered oxides \ch{Li[Li_{1/3}Mn_{2/3}]O2} can deliver over
\SI{300}{mAh/g} through a combination of conventional transition-metal redox
and anionic oxygen redox. Recent understanding of the oxygen loss mechanism has
led to surface coating strategies that suppress O$_2$ evolution.

\subsection{Disordered Rock Salt Oxides}
Fluorine-substituted disordered rock salt oxides \ch{Li_{1.2}TiO_{0.5}F_{1.5}}
operate through Li/vacancy disorder and achieve \SI{>220}{mAh/g}, representing
an entirely new family of cathode materials.

\section{Solid Electrolytes}
Solid electrolytes promise improved energy density (enabling Li-metal anodes),
enhanced safety (non-flammable), and wider electrochemical windows.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of solid electrolyte classes}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Class & Conductivity (S/cm) & E-Window (V) & Stability \\
\midrule
Li$_6$PS$_5$Cl (argyrodite) & \SI{1e-2}{} & 0--5 & Poor vs. oxide cathodes \\
LLZO (garnet) & \SI{1e-3}{} & 0--6 & Good, high interface resistance \\
LiPON & \SI{2e-6}{} & 0--5.5 & Excellent, thin film only \\
PEO-LiTFSI & \SI{1e-4}{} & 0--3.8 & Poor electrochemical window \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Interfacial engineering between the solid electrolyte and electrode is the key
challenge. In situ formation of lithium-ion conducting interphases by addition
of functional molecules to the solid electrolyte has emerged as a promising approach.

\section{Lithium-Sulfur Batteries}
Li-S batteries offer a theoretical energy density of \SI{2600}{Wh/kg}, nearly
ten times that of LIBs, exploiting the two-electron reduction:
\begin{equation}
\ch{S8 + 16Li^+ + 16e^- -> 8Li2S}
\end{equation}
The polysulfide shuttle—whereby soluble \ch{Li2S_n} intermediates diffuse to the
lithium anode—remains the primary cause of capacity fade. Recent strategies include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Polar hosts (\ch{MoS2}, \ch{MXenes}) that chemically anchor polysulfides
  \item Single-atom catalysts that accelerate polysulfide oxidation/reduction
  \item Ether-free electrolytes that suppress polysulfide dissolution
\end{itemize}

\section{Sodium-Ion Batteries}
Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) are attracting considerable attention as a lower-cost
alternative to LIBs for stationary energy storage, given the geological abundance
of sodium. Hard carbon anodes derived from pyrolysis of biomass achieve reversible
capacities of \SI{350}{mAh/g}. Layered oxide cathodes \ch{Na_{0.7}MnO_2} and
Prussian blue analogs offer stable cycling at low cost.

\section{Electrolyte Chemistry}
Localized high-concentration electrolytes (LHCEs) containing co-solvents that
solvate the diluent but not Li\textsuperscript{+} achieve the favorable SEI
chemistry of high-concentration electrolytes while maintaining low viscosity and
acceptable cost. Fluorinated co-solvents (BTFE, TTE) are most effective.

\section{Conclusions}
Battery chemistry is advancing along multiple fronts simultaneously. Solid-state
batteries represent the most transformative near-term opportunity, but materials
challenges at interfaces must be overcome. Sodium-ion technology is reaching
